// Writing the modifier on BOTH sides of the decorator is refused.
//
// Both orders are accepted individually, which raises the question of what two
// disagreeing modifiers on one declaration would mean. Nothing coherent — so it
// is an error, and the diagnostic names both words rather than silently
// preferring one. Preferring one would make `private @extern(...) public fun f()`
// compile as whichever the parser happened to read last, and that is precisely
// the modifier-silently-ignored failure this whole feature was built to remove.
private @extern("i64 llabs(i64)") internal fun _twice(v: Int): Int

IO.println(_twice(-1))
